course: dictionary
lesson: 13260
title: Gloating and unkind pleasure
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Gloating is showing that you are pleased about your own win, or about someone else's bad luck, in a smug and unkind way. «Tom kept gloating over his win, and his friend looked upset.» The clue is how Tom acts: he is rubbing it in, not just feeling happy.
explain: Gloat is a verb, so you can gloat, he gloats, she gloated, or they are gloating. Gloating can also be a noun, as in «No gloating, please.» Look at the words around it: smirking, rubbing it in or laughing at someone's loss all show that gloating is nasty, not friendly.
fact: Gloat means to show you are pleased about your own success, or about someone else's bad luck, in a smug, unkind way.
fact: A gloating look or voice shows this nasty pleasure.
fact: You can gloat over or gloat about something: «She gloated over her win.»
fact: In games, gloating is bad sportsmanship, because it rubs in someone else's loss.
fact: Villains in stories often gloat when they think they have won.
exercise: «Tom kept gloating over his win, and his friend looked upset.» What does gloating mean here? | Saying sorry for a mistake | Running very fast | *Showing smug, unkind pleasure | Feeling very tired after a long race
exercise: «The villain laughed and gloated when the hero fell.» Find and copy one word that means showing smug pleasure at someone else's bad luck. | villain | fell | *gloated | laughed
exercise: Why is gloating bad sportsmanship? | It shows you are being fair to the other team | It is what good players do after a game | It helps the losing team feel better | *It is unkind and rubs in someone else's loss
test: «Sam stopped gloating when he saw how upset Mia was.» What does gloating mean? | Making a loud noise | Sitting quietly and thinking | Helping a friend with homework | *Showing smug, unkind pleasure
test: Which phrase means being pleased about someone's bad luck in a smug way? | Shake hands | *Gloat over | Say well done | Offer help
exam: Which sentence uses gloating as a noun? | «The gloated boy went home.» | «He gloats very quickly.» | *«No gloating, please, we all tried hard.» | «She is gloating about her cake.»
